<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Jewish–Roman War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/66">66</a>–73</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Great Jewish Revolt · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Jewish–Roman War was the revolt of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a> against Roman rule from 66 to 73 CE. Vespasian and then his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus">Titus</a> reconquered the province; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> fell after a long siege in 70 CE and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> was destroyed, and the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masada">Masada</a> was taken in 73 or 74. The war is described in detail by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-jewish-roman-war.jpg" alt="First Jewish–Roman War" width="320" height="178" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: בית השלום · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arch_of_Titus_Menorah.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a> fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> in 63 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> intervened in a succession war between the brothers Hyrcanus and Aristobolus. He entered the Temple's Holy of Holies, which only the High Priest was permitted to do, abolished the Jewish monarchy and made Hyrcanus High Priest alone. Judaea later became a province ruled directly by governors, marked by oppression, social divisions and rising religious and ethnic tension. Most scholars see the revolt as an expression of Jewish nationalism, partly inspired by the successful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maccabean-revolt">Maccabean revolt</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a>. In May 66 violence broke out in Caesarea after a Greek sacrificed birds at the entrance of a synagogue, and Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gessius-florus">Gessius Florus</a> then looted the Temple treasury and massacred residents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Rebels killed the Roman garrison in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. Cestius Gallus, the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, invaded but was defeated at Bethoron, and a provisional government led by the former High Priest Ananus ben Ananus appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> commander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a>. In 67 Vespasian invaded Galilee and captured Yodfat, Tarichaea and Gamla. Refugees and rebels crowded into Jerusalem, where a three-way factional war developed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleazar-ben-simon">Eleazar ben Simon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-gischala">John of Gischala</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bar-giora">Simon bar Giora</a>. Nero died in 68, and when the legions proclaimed Vespasian emperor he left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><p>His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus">Titus</a> returned in the winter of 69 and 70 with more than 48,000 troops and began the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in the spring of 70, when the city was swollen with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passover</a> pilgrims. Jerusalem fell in the summer of 70, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> destroyed the Temple and razed the city. Titus and Vespasian held a triumph in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 71. Roman forces under Sextus Lucilius Bassus took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodium">Herodium</a> and Machaerus, and the last stronghold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masada">Masada</a>, fell in 73 or 74.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> died in battles, sieges and famine, were displaced or were sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> claimed that 1.1 million died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, a figure widely considered exaggerated. The aristocracy, including the High Priesthood, collapsed, as did the Sanhedrin. Legio X Fretensis garrisoned Jerusalem for nearly two centuries, and the Flavian dynasty commemorated the victory in monuments and coinage and imposed a punitive tax on all Jews.</p><p>The destruction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> ended sacrifices and the High Priesthood, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadducees">Sadducees</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essenes">Essenes</a> disappeared from the record. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharisees">Pharisees</a>, who had largely opposed the revolt, survived, and the rabbinic sages emerged as leaders and established a centre at Yavneh. The revolt is seen as one stage in the gradual separation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>. Two further Jewish revolts against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> followed in the second century, among them the Bar Kokhba Revolt of 132 to 136. The main source for the war is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>, who led the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> and was later held captive and then received Roman citizenship.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First Jewish–Roman War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After a siege of probably two to three months, Roman forces breached the wall of Masada, the last rebel stronghold of the <b>First Jewish–Roman War</b>, with a battering ram.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-16">16 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>During the <b>First Jewish–Roman War</b>, Roman legions under Titus retook Jerusalem and destroyed much of the city and the Second Temple.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-30">30 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
